Date: Wed, 31 Mar 93 09:50:00 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: MEDIA: Challenge public radio!

My local public radio station, WBEZ, is currently holding a pledge drive. 
Although I listen to it a lot, I am ignoring its pleas for donations.  And
I do so with a clear conscience.  Why?  Because I am already taxed to
support public radio, because WBEZ unfairly refuses to recognize that I
have already given at the office, and because I think it is bad for public
radio--and for the public it is supposed to serve--for it to accept tax
dollars.  I'd like to explain these reasons more fully, and encourage those
who read this to do as I have done: contact your public radio station and
challenge their use of tax dollars.

I already support public radio involuntarily, as it receives tax dollars
through the federal government.  Many states also contribute tax funds to
public radio.  So when my local station asks me to pay for the broadcasts
that I enjoy, I can decline to do so with a clear conscience.  Now, they
would no doubt tell me that my donation is necessary to make up the
difference between what they get from taxes and their overall operating
expenses.  But I can simply retort that I am putting myself in that class
of persons who supposedly justify public radio's use of tax funds: those
who benefit from its services without paying.  And I am a happy free-rider,
because I think I merit recompense for the indignity of being forced to pay
for services I have not requested and do not always want.

WBEZ, like other public radio stations I have listened to, refuses to
recognize that I am already paying for its services.  I object to having my
support unrecognized.  Worse yet, public radio's silence about its use of
tax funds constitutes a form of false advertising.  Especially during
pledge drives, listeners deserve to hear about public radio's potential
conflicts of interest.

It disserves public radio stations to take tax dollars.  How can they
report objectively about the federal government's budgetary problems and
the proper uses of tax funds if they themselves suckle off the State's
teat?  Public radio is reputed to have a left-liberal bias.  Perhaps its
addiction to tax dollars explains this bias.  In any case, I think it would
be best for public radio, and the public it serves, to eliminate this
conflict of interest by refusing to accept tax dollars.

I thus called up WBEZ and explained why I would not respond to their plea
for funds.  I also requested that they regularly announce the sources and
amounts of the tax dollars they receive.  I encourage all of you to send a
similar message to your local public radio station.  Do the same for public
TV, too!  All the same arguments apply.

Together we can help to bring about a purely private public broadcasting
sector.  This would not only eliminate an unjust (and to my eyes
unconstitutional) use of tax funds; it would also foster more objective
reporting about the proper scope of statism.

You may reproduce this message freely.

