From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Nietzsche and the Nazis

Krzys' writes:

> A comment on Phil's comment -- I firmly believe that the German 
> philosophy of the 19th century was responsible for both the 
> German and Russian totalitarianism of the 20th century.
> Kant, Marx, and to a lesser degree Hegel and Nietsche.
> Krzys'

I'm not a philosopher, though I often play one on this list.


While Krzys' said "lesser degree" with respect to Nietzsche, let me
say a few words in defense of him.

Nietzsche was dead for 25 or 30 years before the rise of Hitler. The
idea that Nietzsche was central to Nazi philosophy seems to come from
several directions:

* his use of the term "superman" (ubermensch) was adopted by Nazis.
Nietzsche's meaning was much closer to the Extropian sense. (However,
the term has been so tainted by the Nazi association and by the comic
book, that it can no longer be used.)

* he was a friend of Wagner's until around 1880 or so, when he
repudiated the views of Wagner.

* his sister was extremely anti-Jewish and wrote several pieces after
her brother's death linkin his philosophy to anti-Semitism.

* in the first world war, the German high command distributed some of
Nietzsche's writings to soldiers, as a kind of morale booster.

Anyway, Nietzsche was a profoundly original and gifted philosopher
with a very "Extropian" slant. He was no totalitarian--I would be
shocked to hear that Nietzsche was ever widely read in Russia, except
surreptitiously. 

I urge you all to read some of his stuff.

-Tim


