From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: Speech for the defense

This is a speach for the defence in the class-action lawsuit now
pending against me on Extropians.
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Several years ago, I was at a dinner party given by a professor of
mine.  During the after-dinner conversation, his young son decided
that he was *not* going to go to bed, and threw himself face-down on
the floor in the midst of the guests.  The professor's wife then
inquired from the professor what reward might be given to the son to
induce him to go to bed.  I immediately visualized taking off my belt
and giving the child a vigorous swat across the buttocks, but decided
that it might be impolitic to suggest this as a motivational device to
the parents.

But it was one of those events which crystalized something that had
been sitting in the back of my mind for a long time...

Most of you are probably too young to remember this, but my upbringing
and my parent's upbringing spanned the period when the "traditional"
discipline-oriented system of child-raising was replaced by the "Dr.
Spock" system of child-raising (somewhere around 1960).  (Actually, I
don't know whether I'm reporting on what Dr. Spock really recommended,
or what it was corrupted into by the public.)  The central tenent of
the Dr. Spock method was that the parents should never make the child
do something that he didn't want to do; that all "discipline" should
be based on reasoning with the child why he *really wanted* to do
whatever-it-was, and on bribes to secure cooperation.  The Spock
system seems to remain the norm in American culture up to the present
day, except in some backwaters like Asian immigrants.

Of course, the result was the Spock-children were unruly, selfish,
undisciplined, and generally intolerable to be around.  Not
surprisingly, in the 60's, when they became teenagers, they formed the
incredibly self-centered hippie movement; during the 70's they formed
the "Me Decade"; and in the 80's became yuppies who had a hard time
distinguishing conspicuous consumption from productive work, and
distinguishing necessary but painful financial restructuring from
simple financial banditry.  For the 90's, let me but quote: "Fewer
than half of all adult Americans today regard the idea of sacrifice
for others as a positive moral virtue."

Spock-children do have the advantage that they are more creative and
independent than preceeding generations, but they tend to not have the
self-discipline to turn their creativity into actual production, since
they have no practice controlling their short-term desires.

As someone here has noticed, the old discipline-based system was
simply beating children until they were "good".  Which is quite true,
but it *did* produce children that one could stand being around, and
who could work together to perform truly impressive feats.

In short, I claim not only that my original statement is correct, but
that it is logically necessary -- "I don't waaana!" is the primal
scream of personal liberation, when the individual first claims his
right to refuse to sacrifice his interests and comfort for the "common
good".

Dale

